📘 STRICT EXECUTION‑TIME RESOURCE‑CONSULTATION RULE (MEDIUM VERSION)
1. Execution begins in one of two states: EXECUTION_TASK_NEW or EXECUTION_TASK_CONTINUATION.  
Cursor MUST determine its state before generating or executing any code.

2. State Definitions
EXECUTION_TASK_NEW
Cursor MUST classify the execution as a new task when the user request involves any of the following:

Creating a new feature

Creating a new role

Creating or modifying a subsystem

Adding functionality not previously planned

Pivoting to a different part of the project

Performing any design, idiom, or structural decision not already covered by an existing plan

Executing code without an existing validated plan for the current request

If any of these conditions apply, Cursor MUST enter EXECUTION_TASK_NEW.

EXECUTION_TASK_CONTINUATION
Cursor MUST classify the execution as a continuation when:

It is completing a plan already created for the current task

It is refining or adjusting code it already generated for the current task

It is continuing execution within the same feature, role, or subsystem without pivoting

It is completing work that has already had resource consultation performed

If all of these conditions apply, Cursor MUST enter EXECUTION_TASK_CONTINUATION.

3. Required Actions Per State
If state = EXECUTION_TASK_NEW
Cursor MUST:

Consult all required resources:

zen_of_ansible

ansible-content-best-practices

All execution-environment-* resources

Perform this consultation before generating or executing any code.

NEVER rely on internal heuristics to skip consultation.

After consultation, execute the requested code or generation action.

Emit a mandatory one‑sentence audit comment in the output:
“# Execution Audit: consulted resources because this is a new execution task.”

Cursor MUST NOT:

Execute or generate code before consultation

Assume prior consultation applies

Skip consultation for any reason

If state = EXECUTION_TASK_CONTINUATION
Cursor MUST:

NOT re‑consult any resources.

Continue execution using the previously validated context.

Emit a mandatory one‑sentence audit comment in the output:
“# Execution Audit: did not re-consult resources because this is a continuation of the same task.”

Cursor MUST NOT:

Treat continuation as a new task

Re-fetch or re-consult resources

Re-plan unless the user request introduces a new task condition

4. Absolute Prohibitions
Cursor MUST NOT:

Decide on its own that resources are “not needed”

Skip consultation during new tasks

Re-consult during continuation

Use internal heuristics to override this rule

Execute code without producing the required audit comment